Z||000000||dgroves||12-05-2001||11:40 AM||||I was reading the thread posted by Mike Murphy and the replies from "Lar", Gunsil etc. Very interesting stuff as I am new to the Fiero "Scene." The question I have concerns the body design. Were the esthetics done at GM or was that engineering task accomplished at an outside design firm? The car has such an "European" flavor, my curiosity was aroused. Perhaps Mr. Aldikacti steered his styling engineers toward this end. Can anyone contribute? ||||reg||3
Z||000001||MinnGreenGT||12-05-2001||11:51 AM||||I belive that all the design was done by the Pontiac Divison at GM... I've seen pics of the design studio and clay mockups- but don't recall seeing any info about who was working on it and where.  Some of the early Fieros had "Body by Fischer" on the inside door sills... if that means anything to you...?<P>------------------<BR>              <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/GreenGT6.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fformula88||12-05-2001||12:48 PM||||It was done by Pontiac at their advanced design studio (II ? ) after the car was made an official program.  Hulki and the others had a basic form/shape down before this as they made the original clay versions and a prototype to try and sell it to the corporate execs.  The clays were done at Pontiac if I am not mistaken, but the prototype was built off-site.  Then the design studio (please help, was the head guy Stumpel?) cleaned it up and made it the car you see today.  BTW, the prototype was also built at an outside firm, but the mockup was done at Pontiac.<P>If only I was home I could get more details right out of the book.  If your interested, get a copy of Gary Witzenburg's Fiero book from  <A HREF="http://www.fierostore.com" TARGET=_blank>www.fierostore.com</A> .  Its a fascinating read for Fiero nuts!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>John  <BR>AOLIM: Fformula88<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/formulasig.JPG">           <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">    <P>1988 Fiero Formula<BR>1985 Fiero SE 2M6||||reg||1
Z||000003||ChopTop||12-05-2001||03:58 PM||||Gary Witzenberg(sp?) book is a good book for Fiero fans(and this is what I'm basing the below statements on).  However, it doesn't really address why the Fiero was designed the way it was.  It gives the impression the car designed itself.  Pontiac always wanted a sports car and we were having a gas crisis.  Pontiac designers needed a car for an upcoming GM product planning meeting (late 70s?).  It was decided that while Americans were concerned about the gas crisis, they also still cared about what they were seen in.  The goal was a small good-looking two-seater commuter car, it's arguable whether Pontiac used this as an opportunity to get their own sports car, though I don't think if can be overlooked that a small block fits into the engine bay with no major modifications.  Initially the engine was proposed to be upfront, but the design of the Ford EXP convinced them it wasn't the way to go.  Moving the motor to the rear gave a much lower hoodline and better aerodynamics.  I believe a Pontiac engineer proposed the initial design of the Fiero and I think Hulki became involved either during, or shortly after the basic design had been established.  A running clay mock up was produced, and won approval at the product planning meeting.  That's when the P-car program officially began.  Hulki is crediting for keeping the program alive, even when it was ordered dropped several times.<BR>The design has been attributed to the Fiat X19, The fact it more closely resembles a Ferrari is what help sell it.  The Fiero is often criticized, but I believe Pontiac did an excellent job with the limited funding that was available.   <p>[This message has been edited by ChopTop (edited 12-05-2001).]||||reg||1
